You are designing a rainwater harvesting system for a modern rooftop. 
Along the edge of the rooftop, there are n vertical rods of varying heights installed,
all aligned in a straight row. The position of each rod corresponds to its index on the 
rooftop, and its height is given by an integer array height[].

When it rains, water can collect between any two rods, bounded by the shorter rod 
and the distance between them (like a gutter formed by two rods and the roof surface).

Your goal is to find two rods that, when connected with a water-proof glass at the 
bottom (the roof), can hold the maximum volume of water between them without 
overflowing.

Write a function that returns the maximum amount of rainwater that can be collected 
between any two rods.

Sample Input and Output:

input=
6
10 1 1 1 1 10
output=50

input=
7
1 1 50 1 1 1 1
output=6

*/

import java.util.*;
public class ContainerWithMostWater 
{
    public static int maxArea(int[] height)
	{
        int left = 0;
        int right = height.length - 1;
        int maxArea = 0;

        while (left < right)
		{
            // Calculate area
            int h = Math.min(height[left], height[right]);
            int w = right - left;
            int area = h * w;
            maxArea = Math.max(maxArea, area);

            // Move the pointer pointing to the shorter line
            if (height[left] < height[right]) 
			{
                left++;
            }
			else 
			{
                right--;
            }
        }

        return maxArea;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) 
	{
		Scanner s=new Scanner(System.in);
		int n=s.nextInt();
        int[] height = new int[n];

		for(int i=0;i<n;i++)
		{
			height[i]=s.nextInt();
		}
        System.out.println(maxArea(height));
    }
}


